import json

with open('complete-qa.json') as f:
    data = json.load(f)

def write_empty(idx, question, direct_answer, intent, z3_secs, sp, kp):
    q = data['questions'][idx]
    q['question'] = question
    q['direct_answer'] = direct_answer
    q['interviewer_intent'] = intent
    q['answer']['sections'] = z3_secs + [
        {"type":"speakable_answer","title":"How to Answer This Verbally","content":sp},
        {"type":"key_points","title":"Key Points","content":kp}
    ]

# Q18: Spring Data JPA Repository Methods
write_empty(18,
    "How do Spring Data JPA repository methods work — findBy, @Query, native queries, and derived methods?",
    "Spring Data JPA generates query implementations from method names (derived queries), JPQL via @Query, or native SQL via @Query(nativeQuery=true). Derived queries parse method names like findByStatusAndCreatedAtAfter into JPQL. @Query gives full control over the query. Native queries bypass JPQL for database-specific SQL.",
    {"testing":"Whether the candidate understands the three query definition approaches and their trade-offs.","common_mistake":"Creating long derived method names like findByStatusAndCityAndCreatedAtAfterAndPriceLessThan instead of using @Query for complex queries.","to_stand_out":"Explain the query derivation mechanism, mention @Param for named parameters, and discuss when derived queries become unmaintainable."},
    [
        {"type":"overview","title":"Spring Data Writes Your SQL \u2014 Until the Method Name Becomes Longer Than the Query",
         "content":"Spring Data JPA's killer feature is **derived query methods** \u2014 declare `findByStatusAndCreatedAtAfter(String status, LocalDate date)` and Spring generates the JPQL at startup. No implementation class, no SQL, no boilerplate. The query is parsed from the method name using a well-defined naming convention.\n\nThis works beautifully for simple queries. It breaks down when method names become sentences: `findByStatusAndCityAndCreatedAtAfterAndPriceLessThanOrderByCreatedAtDesc`. At this point, the method name is longer than the JPQL it would replace, harder to read, and impossible to refactor without understanding the naming convention.\n\n**`@Query` is the escape hatch** \u2014 write the JPQL explicitly and name the method anything you want. For database-specific features (window functions, CTEs, full-text search), `@Query(nativeQuery = true)` drops to raw SQL. The trade-off: native queries bypass entity management and cache integration. Each approach has a sweet spot."},
        {"type":"comparison_table","title":"Derived vs @Query vs Native \u2014 When Each Approach Fits",
         "content":"Match the query complexity to the definition approach.\n\n| Aspect | Derived Method | @Query (JPQL) | @Query (Native) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Definition** | Method name convention | Explicit JPQL string | Raw SQL string |\n| **Readability** | Good for 1-2 conditions | Always clear | SQL-familiar |\n| **Validation** | At startup (name parsing) | At startup (JPQL parsing) | At runtime only |\n| **Portability** | Database-agnostic | Database-agnostic | Database-specific |\n| **Complex joins** | Limited | Full JPQL support | Full SQL support |\n| **Cache integration** | Full | Full | Bypasses L2 |\n| **Best for** | Simple findBy with 1-2 fields | Joins, aggregations, custom logic | Window functions, CTEs, full-text |\n\n**Derived queries validate at startup** \u2014 a typo in the method name causes a startup failure, which is safer than a runtime SQL error. `@Query` with JPQL also validates at startup. Native queries are only validated when first executed, so errors hide until the code path is hit in production."},
    ],
    "Spring Data JPA gives you three ways to define queries. **Derived methods** parse the query from the method name \u2014 `findByStatus(String status)` generates JPQL automatically. Great for simple lookups, but method names become unreadable past two or three conditions.\n\n**@Query with JPQL** is the sweet spot for anything complex \u2014 explicit query, readable method name, database-agnostic, validated at startup.\n\n**Native queries** give full SQL power but bypass entity management and cache integration. Use only for database-specific features like window functions or full-text search.\n\nDerived queries and JPQL @Query both validate at startup \u2014 a bad method name or JPQL syntax fails fast. Native queries only validate at runtime, so errors hide until the code path is hit.\n\nIn practice, I use derived queries for simple single-field lookups, @Query for anything with joins or multiple conditions, and native SQL only when JPQL can't express what I need.",
    "- **Derived queries validate at startup, native queries don't** \u2014 a typo in `findByStatsu` causes an immediate startup failure; a typo in a native SQL string hides until the query is first executed at runtime.\n- **Method names longer than the query they replace are a code smell** \u2014 `findByStatusAndCityAndDateAfterAndPriceLessThan` should be replaced with `@Query` for readability and maintainability.\n- **@Param binds named parameters in @Query** \u2014 `@Query(\"SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.email = :email\")` with `@Param(\"email\")` prevents positional parameter confusion in complex queries.\n- **Native queries bypass L2 cache and entity lifecycle** \u2014 results aren't cached, `@PreUpdate` callbacks don't fire on bulk operations, and the persistence context may hold stale data.\n- **Spring Data validates derived query method names against the entity metamodel** \u2014 `findByNonExistentField` fails at startup with a clear error; this compile-time-like safety is lost with @Query strings."
)

# Q19: Pagination and Sorting
write_empty(19,
    "How do you implement pagination and sorting with Spring Data JPA using Pageable, Page, and Slice?",
    "Spring Data JPA accepts Pageable as a method parameter to add pagination and sorting. Page<T> returns results with total element count (requires a COUNT query). Slice<T> returns results with only a hasNext flag (no COUNT). Use Slice for infinite scroll where total count is unnecessary and expensive.",
    {"testing":"Whether the candidate knows the difference between Page and Slice and understands the COUNT query performance impact.","common_mistake":"Always using Page when Slice would suffice \u2014 the COUNT(*) query on large tables can be slower than the actual data query.","to_stand_out":"Explain keyset pagination as an alternative to OFFSET for large datasets, and mention that COUNT queries can be optimized with @Query(countQuery)."},
    [
        {"type":"overview","title":"The COUNT Query Is Often Slower Than the Data Query \u2014 And You Might Not Need It",
         "content":"Every `Page<T>` response triggers **two SQL queries** \u2014 one for the data and one `COUNT(*)` for the total number of matching rows. On a table with millions of rows, the COUNT query can take **longer than the data query** because the database must scan the entire matching set to count it, while the data query stops at the LIMIT.\n\n`Slice<T>` skips the COUNT entirely. It fetches `size + 1` rows \u2014 if it gets more rows than the page size, there's a next page. This makes it ideal for **infinite scroll** interfaces where \"page 3 of 47\" isn't displayed and the total count adds no value.\n\nBoth `Page` and `Slice` accept `Pageable` as input, which combines page number, page size, and sort direction. Spring Data translates this to `OFFSET` and `LIMIT` in the generated SQL. For APIs, expose pagination via request parameters \u2014 `?page=0&size=20&sort=createdAt,desc` maps directly to a `Pageable`."},
        {"type":"step","title":"Custom COUNT Queries and the OFFSET Performance Problem at Scale",
         "content":"When the default COUNT is too slow, `@Query(countQuery = \"...\")` lets you write an optimized count. A common pattern is removing unnecessary JOINs from the count \u2014 if the data query joins three tables, the count might only need one.\n\nThe deeper problem is `OFFSET`-based pagination itself. `OFFSET 10000 LIMIT 20` makes the database **scan and discard 10,000 rows** before returning your 20. At page 500, this is seconds of latency. The alternative is **keyset pagination** (cursor-based) \u2014 `WHERE id > :lastSeenId ORDER BY id LIMIT 20` uses an index seek and reads exactly 20 rows regardless of depth.\n\nSpring Data doesn't have built-in keyset pagination, but you can implement it with `@Query` and a custom `Pageable` that passes the cursor instead of the offset. For APIs with large datasets and deep pagination, this is essential for consistent performance."},
    ],
    "Every `Page<T>` triggers two SQL queries \u2014 one for data, one COUNT(*) for total elements. On large tables, the **COUNT is often slower than the data query** because it scans the entire matching set.\n\n`Slice<T>` skips the COUNT entirely \u2014 it fetches `size + 1` rows to check if there's a next page. Use it for infinite scroll where total count adds no value.\n\n`Pageable` combines page number, size, and sort direction. Spring translates it to OFFSET and LIMIT. For APIs, `?page=0&size=20&sort=createdAt,desc` maps directly.\n\nThe deeper problem is OFFSET pagination at scale. OFFSET 10000 scans and discards 10,000 rows. Keyset pagination with `WHERE id > :cursor` reads exactly 20 rows regardless of depth.\n\nIn practice, I use `Slice` for infinite scroll, `Page` only when total count is displayed, and `@Query(countQuery)` to optimize the COUNT when it can't be avoided.",
    "- **Page triggers a COUNT query, Slice does not** \u2014 `Page<T>` fires two SQL statements; `Slice<T>` fetches size+1 rows to detect next page; use Slice when total count isn't displayed.\n- **COUNT(*) on large tables can be slower than the data query** \u2014 the database scans the entire matching set to count; optimize with `@Query(countQuery)` that removes unnecessary JOINs.\n- **OFFSET pagination degrades linearly with depth** \u2014 OFFSET 10000 LIMIT 20 scans 10,020 rows; keyset pagination with `WHERE id > :cursor` reads exactly 20 rows at any depth.\n- **Pageable maps directly from request parameters** \u2014 `?page=0&size=20&sort=createdAt,desc` is auto-resolved by Spring; defaults can be set with `@PageableDefault(size=20, sort=\"createdAt\")`.\n- **Sort direction matters for index usage** \u2014 `ORDER BY created_at DESC` uses a descending index scan; without a matching index, the database sorts in memory which is expensive on large result sets."
)

# Q20: HikariCP Connection Pooling
write_empty(20,
    "How does HikariCP connection pooling work in Spring Boot, and how do you configure and tune it?",
    "HikariCP is Spring Boot's default connection pool. It maintains a pool of reusable database connections, avoiding the overhead of creating new connections per request. Key settings are maximumPoolSize (default 10), minimumIdle (defaults to maximumPoolSize), and connectionTimeout (30 seconds). Undersizing causes request queuing; oversizing wastes database resources and can reduce throughput due to context switching.",
    {"testing":"Whether the candidate understands connection pool sizing and its impact on application performance.","common_mistake":"Setting maximumPoolSize to 100 thinking more connections means better performance \u2014 database servers perform worse with many concurrent connections due to CPU context switching and lock contention.","to_stand_out":"Mention the formula connections = (2 * CPU cores) + disk spindles, explain leak detection with leakDetectionThreshold, and discuss how @Transactional scope affects connection hold time."},
    [
        {"type":"overview","title":"100 Connections Doesn't Mean 100x Throughput \u2014 It Usually Means Worse Performance",
         "content":"Developers set `maximumPoolSize=100` thinking more connections means more throughput. **The opposite is often true.** A PostgreSQL benchmark by the HikariCP team showed that **a pool of 10 connections outperformed a pool of 100** on the same hardware. More connections means more CPU context switching, more lock contention in the database, and more memory consumed per connection.\n\nThe general formula is **connections = (2 \u00d7 CPU cores) + effective disk spindles.** For an 8-core database server with SSDs, that's roughly 17-20 connections. Going beyond this doesn't increase parallelism \u2014 it increases contention.\n\nHikariCP is Spring Boot's default connection pool. It starts with `minimumIdle` connections (defaults to `maximumPoolSize`) and grows up to `maximumPoolSize` (default 10). When a request needs a connection and none are available, it waits up to `connectionTimeout` (30 seconds) before throwing an exception. **Undersized pools cause request queuing; oversized pools waste database resources and reduce throughput.**"},
        {"type":"step","title":"Critical Configuration Properties and Leak Detection",
         "content":"The most important properties for production tuning affect how long connections are held and how failures are detected.\n\n**`maximumPoolSize`** should match your database's capacity divided by the number of application instances. 4 instances sharing a database with 80 max connections = 20 per instance, minus overhead for admin/migration connections.\n\n**`connectionTimeout`** (default 30s) is how long a thread waits for a connection. In high-traffic APIs, 30 seconds is too long \u2014 the request is already timed out at the load balancer. Set it to 2-5 seconds and fail fast.\n\n**`leakDetectionThreshold`** logs a warning when a connection is held longer than the threshold without being returned. Set it to the expected maximum query time (e.g., 10 seconds). **Connection leaks** \u2014 code that acquires a connection but doesn't close it \u2014 are the most common pool exhaustion cause. HikariCP logs the stack trace of the leak point, making it straightforward to find.\n\n**`@Transactional` scope directly affects connection hold time.** A transaction holds a connection for its entire duration. A `@Transactional` method that calls an external HTTP service holds a database connection while waiting for the response \u2014 wasting pool capacity on network I/O."},
    ],
    "HikariCP is Spring Boot's default connection pool. The most common mistake is setting `maximumPoolSize` to a large number thinking more connections = better performance. **A pool of 10 often outperforms 100** because the database server spends more time context-switching than executing queries.\n\nThe formula is roughly `connections = (2 \u00d7 CPU cores) + disk spindles`. For an 8-core database server, that's about 20 connections total \u2014 divided across application instances.\n\nSet `connectionTimeout` to 2-5 seconds for APIs, not the default 30 \u2014 the request is already dead at the load balancer by then. Use `leakDetectionThreshold` to catch connection leaks \u2014 HikariCP logs the stack trace of code that holds connections too long.\n\nThe hidden connection waster is `@Transactional` scope. A transactional method calling an external HTTP service holds a database connection during the entire network call.\n\nIn practice, I start with `maximumPoolSize=10`, monitor with HikariCP metrics, and only increase based on actual connection wait times.",
    "- **A pool of 10 connections often outperforms 100** \u2014 excessive connections cause CPU context switching and lock contention in the database; the formula is `(2 \u00d7 CPU cores) + disk spindles`.\n- **connectionTimeout default of 30s is too long for APIs** \u2014 set 2-5 seconds to fail fast; the request is already timed out at the load balancer by the time a 30s wait completes.\n- **leakDetectionThreshold logs the stack trace of connection leaks** \u2014 set to expected max query time (e.g., 10s); HikariCP warns when a connection is held without being returned, identifying the exact code path.\n- **@Transactional scope determines connection hold time** \u2014 a transactional method calling an external HTTP service holds a database connection during the entire network wait; move HTTP calls outside the transaction.\n- **maximumPoolSize must account for all application instances** \u2014 4 instances with poolSize=20 each consume 80 database connections; the database's max_connections must accommodate all instances plus admin/migration overhead."
)

# Q21: Flyway/Liquibase Database Migrations
write_empty(21,
    "How do you manage database schema migrations with Flyway or Liquibase in a Spring Boot application?",
    "Flyway and Liquibase manage database schema changes as versioned migration scripts that run automatically at application startup. Flyway uses SQL files (V1__create_users.sql) with a naming convention. Liquibase uses XML/YAML/JSON changelogs. Both track applied migrations in a metadata table to ensure each migration runs exactly once.",
    {"testing":"Whether the candidate understands schema migration versioning and its role in CI/CD pipelines.","common_mistake":"Modifying an already-applied migration script instead of creating a new migration \u2014 Flyway detects the checksum mismatch and refuses to start.","to_stand_out":"Discuss repeatable migrations in Flyway, rollback limitations, and how to handle migration failures in production (out-of-order migrations, baseline)."},
    [
        {"type":"overview","title":"Editing an Applied Migration Breaks Your Application at Startup \u2014 Always Create New Migrations",
         "content":"The most common Flyway mistake is editing a migration script that's already been applied. Flyway stores a **checksum of every applied migration** in its metadata table (`flyway_schema_history`). If the checksum of a file on disk doesn't match the stored checksum, Flyway refuses to start the application. This is a safety mechanism \u2014 applied migrations represent the actual state of the database.\n\nBoth Flyway and Liquibase solve the same problem: **making database schema changes repeatable, versioned, and automated.** Instead of manually running ALTER TABLE scripts on each environment, migration files are part of the codebase, versioned in Git, and run automatically at startup.\n\nFlyway is simpler \u2014 SQL files with a naming convention (`V1__create_users.sql`, `V2__add_email_column.sql`). Liquibase is more powerful \u2014 XML/YAML changelogs with database-agnostic change types and automatic rollback generation. **Spring Boot auto-configures both** \u2014 add the dependency, put migration files in the right directory, and they run at startup."},
        {"type":"step","title":"Flyway Naming Convention, Repeatable Migrations, and Production Recovery",
         "content":"Flyway migration files follow a strict naming convention: **`V{version}__{description}.sql`** \u2014 two underscores between version and description. Versions can be numeric (`V1`, `V2`) or semantic (`V1.1`, `V2.0.1`). Files are applied in version order.\n\n**Repeatable migrations** (`R__create_views.sql`) run every time their checksum changes. Use them for views, stored procedures, or seed data that needs to be recreated when modified \u2014 they always run after versioned migrations.\n\nWhen a migration fails in production, the database is in a **partially migrated state.** Flyway marks the migration as failed. Fix the issue manually, then use `flyway repair` to update the metadata table. In CI/CD pipelines, run `flyway validate` before deployment to catch checksum mismatches early.\n\n**Liquibase's advantage** is automatic rollback generation for simple changes (add column \u2192 drop column) and database-agnostic changeset definitions. Flyway has no built-in rollback \u2014 you write undo migrations manually. For most Spring Boot projects, Flyway's simplicity wins unless you need multi-database support or automatic rollbacks."},
    ],
    "Database migration tools make schema changes versioned, repeatable, and automated. Put SQL files in `db/migration`, and they run at startup.\n\nThe most common mistake is **editing an applied migration.** Flyway stores checksums of every applied migration. Change a file that's already been applied, and the application refuses to start.\n\nFlyway is simpler \u2014 SQL files with a naming convention: `V1__create_users.sql`. Liquibase is more powerful \u2014 XML changelogs with database-agnostic change types and automatic rollback generation.\n\nRepeatable migrations (`R__create_views.sql`) run whenever their content changes \u2014 use for views and stored procedures that need to be recreated.\n\nWhen a migration fails in production, the database is partially migrated. Fix it manually, run `flyway repair` to update metadata, then redeploy.\n\nIn practice, I use Flyway for its simplicity. The naming convention enforces ordering, checksums prevent accidental edits, and it integrates with Spring Boot with zero configuration.",
    "- **Editing an applied migration causes checksum mismatch at startup** \u2014 Flyway stores checksums in `flyway_schema_history`; always create a new versioned migration instead of modifying an existing one.\n- **V{version}__{description}.sql naming convention is strict** \u2014 two underscores between version and description; versions determine execution order; files without the correct prefix are ignored.\n- **Repeatable migrations (R__) run whenever their checksum changes** \u2014 use for views, procedures, and seed data that should be recreated on every modification; they always execute after versioned migrations.\n- **Flyway has no built-in rollback \u2014 Liquibase does** \u2014 Liquibase auto-generates rollbacks for simple changes (add column \u2192 drop column); Flyway requires manual undo scripts or `flyway undo` (Teams edition only).\n- **Run flyway validate in CI before deployment** \u2014 catches checksum mismatches and missing migrations before they fail at startup in production; faster feedback than waiting for application boot."
)

